<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="adenosine 5&apos;-phosphoramidate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-487 &quot;Adenosine 5&apos;-phosphoramidate&quot;| is a natural but uncommon nucleotide that was discovered in |FRAME: TAX-3078| cells |CITS: [7306093]|. The biochemistry of this compound is poorly understood. A study that looked for the compound in cell extracts found it in a range of organisms including |FRAME: TAX-3078|, |FRAME: TAX-3039|, |FRAME: TAX-3562|, |FRAME: TAX-4513|, |FRAME: TAX-44689| and |FRAME: TAX-562| |CITS: [6274307]|. An enzyme that catalyzes the formation of |FRAME: CPD-487| from |FRAME: APS| and |FRAME: AMMONIA| was purified and characterized from chlorella cells |CITS: [6274307]|. The degradation of |FRAME: CPD-487| has been studied in yellow Lupin (|FRAME: TAX-3873|) by using the nucleotide analog |FRAME: CPD-15038|. The study showed that the lupin |FRAME: CPLX-8399| splits the P-S bond in adenosine 5&apos;-phosphorothioate, similarly to the action of |FRAME: AT4G16566| proteins in Arabidopsis |CITS: [21403921]|.</Description>
